# ── Shell-quoting debris ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# An unbalanced quote in a shell command makes the shell read fragments of
# JavaScript as redirection targets, dropping empty files in the repo root
# with names like `d.id)`, `upsert('aee_prospects'`, `{` or plain `NOT`.
#
# Enumerating the punctuation those fragments carry never caught the
# bare-word ones, so the root is allow-listed instead: it holds a small,
# stable set of files, and anything else turning up there is debris.
#
# This block has to stay FIRST. The last matching rule wins, and `!/*/`
# re-includes every root directory — placed after the sections below it
# would undo `node_modules/`, `memory/` and the rest.
/*
# Never hide a directory, so a new one is still picked up normally.
!/*/
# ...with one exception. A name that is literally shell-variable syntax is
# always an unexpanded argument, never a real directory — `git worktree add
# "$WT" <branch>` run where WT was never set creates a whole worktree called
# `$WT` inside the repo, which `git add -A` will then happily commit. This
# has to sit after `!/*/` to override the re-inclusion above.
/$*
/${*
!/.gitignore
!/*.html
!/*.css
!/*.js
!/*.md
!/*.json
!/*.txt
!/*.svg
!/*.ico
!/*.png
!/*.webmanifest

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Editors
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# Node (not used today, but keeps a stray install out of the repo)
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*

# Secrets — never commit these
.env
.env.*
*.pem
# ...except the template, which is the point: it carries the variable
# names and not one value. Listed after the rules above so it overrides
# `.env.*`, and narrow enough that `.env.local` stays ignored.
!.env.example

# Local exports
*.csv
!data/*.csv

# Local agent tooling
.claude-flow/
.swarm/
memory/
